.TITLE "tail"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Displays the end of a file or piped data. By default, it shows the __last 10 lines__, which is ideal for monitoring logs or checking the most recent entries in a file.

.DIV "TLDR"
**tail file**
Displays the last 10 lines of the specified file.
> __file__ the target file

**tail -n number file**
Displays a specific number of lines from the end of the file.
> __-n__ line count flag | __number__ how many lines to show | __file__ target file

**tail -f file**
Keeps the file open and displays new lines as they are added in real-time.
> __-f__ follow flag | __file__ the target file

**tail -c bytes file**
Displays the last specific number of bytes from the file.
> __-c__ byte count flag | __bytes__ how many bytes to show | __file__ target file

.DIV "SUMMARY"
The __tail -f__ command is an essential tool for system administrators to monitor live log files. Combining __tail__ with __grep__ allows you to filter for specific events as they occur in a log.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**linux:head** **linux:cat** **linux:journalctl**

